window.STORIES=[
{id:'lantern',title:'The lantern across the water',category:'FICTION / OBSERVATION',paragraphs:['Every evening, Nia saw a lantern across the harbour. It flashed twice, paused, then flashed again. She imagined a sailor sending a secret message.','One rainy night, she watched from a different bench. The flashes changed. A passing boat revealed the answer: a turning light shone through gaps between old wooden posts.','Nia wrote both explanations in her notebook. The mystery had become a lesson: changing where you stand can change what you think you see.'],question:'What observation helped Nia revise her explanation?',diagram:['A REPEATING PATTERN','A NEW VIEWPOINT','A REVISED EXPLANATION'],symbol:'☼'},
{id:'seed',title:'The seed in the pavement',category:'FICTION / NATURAL SYSTEMS',paragraphs:['On his walk to school, Milo noticed a green shoot in a crack beside the bakery. The baker called it a weed. Milo called it a visitor.','For a week, he sketched its leaves. After rain, the shoot grew taller. During two hot days, its leaves drooped. Milo resisted pulling it up to examine the roots.','Instead, he placed a small marker nearby and kept watching. Some questions, he learned, were better answered by returning than by disturbing.'],question:'Why did Milo choose to keep watching?',diagram:['RAIN ARRIVES','THE SHOOT GROWS','OBSERVATION CONTINUES'],symbol:'❧'},
{id:'bridge',title:'A bridge made of paper',category:'FICTION / DESIGN',paragraphs:['Aya laid a sheet of paper between two books and placed a coin in the middle. The paper sagged. Her first bridge had failed.','She folded the same sheet into a fan and tried again. This time it held several coins. She changed only the fold, keeping the books and the paper the same.','Aya drew the two bridges side by side. The stronger design had used no extra material. Its shape had changed how it carried the load.'],question:'What did Aya keep the same to make her comparison useful?',diagram:['FLAT PAPER','FOLDED PAPER','COMPARE THE LOAD'],symbol:'⌁'},
{id:'clock',title:'The clock that lost a minute',category:'FICTION / MEASUREMENT',paragraphs:['The station clock always seemed slow to Theo. On Monday, he compared it with his watch and found a difference of one minute.','On Tuesday, he checked again at the same hour. The difference was still one minute. His sister suggested borrowing another clock before deciding which one was wrong.','Three clocks agreed with the station. Theo adjusted his watch and kept his notes. A repeated difference was real, but his first explanation had pointed at the wrong clock.'],question:'Why was a third reference useful?',diagram:['NOTICE A DIFFERENCE','CHECK A REFERENCE','REVISE THE CLAIM'],symbol:'◷'},
{id:'map',title:'The map with a missing path',category:'FICTION / EXPLORATION',paragraphs:['Lea followed a printed map to the community garden. At the corner, a fence blocked the path shown on the page.','She walked around the block and found a new entrance beside a mural. Inside, a gardener explained that the old gate had closed during repairs.','Lea marked the new entrance and added a date. A useful map, she decided, should tell you not only where things are, but when someone last checked.'],question:'What detail made Lea’s updated map more useful?',diagram:['AN OLD MAP','A CHANGED ROUTE','A DATED UPDATE'],symbol:'↗'},
{id:'echo',title:'An echo under the arch',category:'FICTION / EXPERIMENT',paragraphs:['Sam clapped beneath a stone arch and heard a quick reply. His friend clapped in the open square and heard none.','They tried again, keeping their claps similar. Beneath the arch, the reply returned. In the square, the sound faded into the afternoon.','They could not measure the delay with their watches. They recorded that limit beside their observations rather than inventing a precise number.'],question:'Why did the friends record what they could not measure?',diagram:['KEEP THE CLAP SIMILAR','CHANGE THE LOCATION','RECORD THE LIMIT'],symbol:'≋'}
];
